<?php endif; ?>

<?php if ($stdioMode && $stdioEnable): ?>
<div class="title"><span class="left"><i class="title fa fa-terminal"></i>SSH Setup for stdio</span></div>
<div style="padding:12px 16px;margin:8px 0;border-left:3px solid #4a90d9;background:rgba(74,144,217,0.08);line-height:1.7;">
stdio over SSH requires key-based authentication from your client machine:<br><br>
1. On your client: <code>ssh-keygen -t ed25519</code> (skip if you already have a key)<br>
2. Install the public key on this server: append <code>~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub</code> (client) to <code>/root/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> (server)<br>
3. Enable SSH: Unraid Settings → Management Access → SSH<?php if ($sshPort !== '22'): ?> (port <?=htmlspecialchars($sshPort)?>)<?php endif; ?><br>
4. Test: <code>ssh -T -o BatchMode=yes <?php if ($sshPort !== '22'): ?>-p <?=htmlspecialchars($sshPort)?> <?php endif; ?>root@<?=htmlspecialchars($serverHost)?> true</code><br><br>
<b>Security note:</b> SSH-stdio grants root shell access to anyone holding the key.
streamable-http with a bearer token is the smaller attack surface — prefer it for
always-on use, and set Enable stdio Transport back to No when you're done.
</div>
